13.1  Where environment variables come from

TL;DR
  - Every process gets a copy of its parent's environment: a list of NAME=value
    strings.
  - export NAME=value sets it for the current shell and the programs it starts,
    not for its parent or for other terminals.
  - To keep it, put the export in a startup file (13.2). For graphical apps on
    systemd desktops, use a file in ~/.config/environment.d/.

      printenv LANG
      export EDITOR=nvim
      LANG=C sort names.txt    # set for one command only

On your machine
  SHELL     /bin/bash
  HOME      /home/alice
  LANG      en_US.UTF-8
  EDITOR    nvim
  TERM      xterm-256color
  Login     /bin/zsh (SHELL says /bin/bash: changed since login?)
  In all    8 variables set; only well-known ones are shown here

Pitfalls
  - A variable set without export stays in the shell: the programs it starts
    don't see it.
  - A change never reaches programs already running, nor other terminals: open a
    new one, or source ~/.bashrc.
  - systemd services don't read your shell files: give them variables with
    Environment= in a drop-in (see 11.3).
  - sudo resets most of the environment. sudo -E keeps it, when sudoers allows
    it.
  - Variables are visible to every process you run, in /proc/PID/environ: avoid
    keeping secrets there for long.

Deep dive

  ▾ Where does the first environment come from?

    At login, PAM and the login program set HOME, USER, SHELL and a default
    PATH; pam_env adds what's in /etc/environment. The login shell then runs its
    startup files, which add more. Desktop sessions started by systemd also read
    environment.d files. Every program after that inherits the result.

  ▾ What do LANG and LC_ALL do?

    They choose the language of messages, the format of numbers and dates, the
    sort order and the character encoding. LC_ALL overrides everything, LANG is
    the fallback. locale shows the current values, and LC_ALL=C gives scripts
    stable, byte-based behaviour.

Related
  Commands  env, printenv, export
  Paths     /etc/environment, ~/.config/environment.d/
  See also  idk 13.2 Shell startup files
  See also  idk 12.3 PATH and command resolution
